./docs/helix/02-design/system-design/SD-011-pipeline-performance-model.md:480:**Mechanism**: `scripts/perf-regression-check.sh` ensures a cached fio hardware baseline exists for the current disk identity, runs `integrated_pipeline_bench --output json`, and compares the emitted `io_efficiency` ratios against `scripts/fixtures/perf-ratchets.json`. Ratchet floors ensure these ratios never regress below a known-good minimum after applying the configured tolerance band.
./docs/helix/02-design/system-design/SD-011-pipeline-performance-model.md:488:**Stable sampling policy**: ratchet checks and `--update-ratchets` use `PERF_RATCHET_SAMPLE_COUNT`, defaulting to `3`. For `materialize_io_efficiency` and `scan_io_efficiency`, the script writes one benchmark JSON per sample and stores the median ratio in the final `pipeline_bench_results.json`; `produce_io_efficiency` and `combined_io_efficiency` continue to use the latest report from the same run set. The final JSON includes `io_efficiency_sampling` metadata so evidence artifacts record the policy, sample count, per-sample ratio values, and aggregated ratio names. Operators may set `PERF_RATCHET_SAMPLE_COUNT=1` only for diagnostic single-sample runs; release-stable ratchet updates must use the default median policy or a documented exception.
./docs/helix/02-design/system-design/SD-011-pipeline-performance-model.md:490:**Variance evidence**: on 2026-04-19, same-host `/dev/sde` runs under `io_baseline_hash=67d4a01f1683` showed `materialize_io_efficiency` ranging from `0.194` to `0.358` and `scan_io_efficiency` ranging from `0.075` to `0.838` while reconciling `niflheim-1d191526`. Single-sample ratchet updates for these two non-WAL ratios are therefore not release-stable. See `docs/helix/04-build/evidence/IP-010/perf/20260419T230422Z-niflheim-1d191526-checkpoint-a-reconciliation.md`.
